Supreme People's Court, Supreme People's Procuratorate, China Coast Guard

Notice on Issuing the Summary of the Symposium on Cracking Down on Sea Sand-related Crimes and Illegal Activities

Fa Fa [2023] No. 9

High People's Courts and People's Procuratorates of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; Military Courts and Military Procuratorates of the People's Liberation Army; Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region, People's Procuratorate of Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps; all branches and directly subordinate bureaus of the China Coast Guard; coast guard bureaus of coastal provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government:

In order to thoroughly study and implement Xi Jinping Thought on Socialism with Chinese Characteristics for a New Era, fully implement Xi Jinping Thought on Ecological Civilization and Xi Jinping Thought on the Rule of Law, crack down on sea sand-related crimes and illegal activities in accordance with the law, and effectively protect the marine ecological environment and the security of mineral resources, in accordance with the Criminal Law of the People's Republic of China, the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China and other legal provisions, the Supreme People's Court, the Supreme People's Procuratorate and the China Coast Guard have formulated the Summary of the Symposium on Cracking Down on Sea Sand-related Crimes and Illegal Activities, which is hereby issued. Please conscientiously implement it in light of the actual circumstances. Any problems encountered during implementation shall be reported in a timely manner to the Supreme People's Court, the Supreme People's Procuratorate and the China Coast Guard respectively.

June 6, 2023

Supreme People's Court, Supreme People's Procuratorate, China Coast Guard

Summary of the Symposium on Cracking Down on Sea Sand-related Crimes and Illegal Activities

The 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China made a strategic deployment of "developing the marine economy, protecting the marine ecological environment, and accelerating the construction of a maritime power", taking the construction of a maritime power as an organic component and an important task in promoting Chinese-style modernization. Facing the severe and complex marine situation and international situation, as a major country in maritime trade and shipping, cracking down on marine-related illegal and criminal activities in accordance with the law and accelerating the construction of marine rule of law are inevitable requirements for thoroughly studying and implementing Xi Jinping Thought on Socialism with Chinese Characteristics for a New Era, implementing Xi Jinping Thought on Ecological Civilization and Xi Jinping Thought on the Rule of Law, and improving the foreign-related legal system. In July 2022 and February 2023, the Supreme People's Court, the Supreme People's Procuratorate and the China Coast Guard successively held symposiums in Fujian, Guangdong, Hainan and Zhejiang provinces, analyzing and judging the current severe situation of sea sand-related crimes and illegal activities, summarizing and exchanging experiences and practices in handling criminal cases involving sea sand, studying and discussing difficult issues in case handling, and reaching consensus on cracking down on sea sand-related crimes and illegal activities and unifying the application of legal standards by people's courts, people's procuratorates and coast guard agencies.

The symposium pointed out that in recent years, illegal and criminal activities involving sea sand have been frequent and high, threatening the safety of the marine ecological environment, giving rise to black and evil forces at sea, endangering the safety of construction projects, affecting the safety of maritime navigation, and having great social harm. The symposium requested that all departments should effectively improve their political stance, keep in mind the "great affairs of the country", closely focus on the overall work of the Party and the state, and build a solid judicial and enforcement barrier for maintaining the safety of the marine ecological environment and sea sand resources with the strictest system and the strictest rule of law. The symposium emphasized that all departments should correctly understand and accurately apply the Criminal Law and the "Interpretation of the Supreme People's Court and the Supreme People's Procuratorate on Several Issues Concerning the Application of Law in Handling Criminal Cases of Illegal Mining and Destructive Mining" (Fa Shi [2016] No. 25, hereinafter referred to as the "Illegal Mining Interpretation"), the "Opinions of the Supreme People's Court on Giving Full Play to the Role of Environmental Resources Adjudication Functions and Punishing Crimes of Stealing Mineral Resources in Accordance with the Law" (Fa Fa [2022] No. 19) and other provisions, adhere to the criminal policy of combining leniency and severity, unify the standards of law enforcement and justice, increase the punishment of sea sand-related crimes and illegal activities in accordance with the law, and effectively protect the safety of the marine ecological environment and mineral resources. The summary is hereby formed as follows.

I. Application of Charges

1. If a person, without obtaining a sea area use right certificate for sea sand mining and without obtaining a mining license, excavates sea sand in the internal waters or territorial sea of the People's Republic of China, and the circumstances meet the provisions of Article 343, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law and Articles 2 and 3 of the Illegal Mining Interpretation, he shall be convicted and punished for the crime of illegal mining.

For the act prescribed in the preceding paragraph committed in the contiguous zone, exclusive economic zone, continental shelf and other sea areas under the jurisdiction of the People's Republic of China, if the criminal liability is pursued under the Criminal Law of our country, the case shall be convicted and punished by reference to the preceding paragraph.

2. If any of the following circumstances exists, the shipowner or captain who transships and transports sea sand shall be convicted and punished for the crime of illegal mining in accordance with Article 343, Paragraph 1 of the Criminal Law:

(1) Having prior collusion with the criminal of illegal sea sand excavation, he directs or pilots the sand transport vessel to the designated sea area to directly transship and transport sea sand from the sand mining vessel;

(2) Without prior collusion with the criminal of illegal sea sand excavation, but being employed by him, he directs or pilots the sand transport vessel to the designated sea area, and while the illegal sand mining is still ongoing, knowing it is illegally excavated sea sand, still directly transships and transports sea sand from the sand mining vessel;

(3) Without prior collusion with the criminal of illegal sea sand excavation and without being employed by him, while the illegal sand mining is still ongoing, knowing it is illegally excavated sea sand, he temporarily agrees with the criminal on the time and place, and directly transships and transports sea sand from the sand mining vessel.

If any of the following circumstances exists, the shipowner or captain who transships and transports sea sand shall be convicted and punished for the crime of concealing or covering up criminal proceeds in accordance with Article 312 of the Criminal Law:

(1) Without prior collusion with the criminal of illegal sea sand excavation, he directs or pilots the sand transport vessel to the relevant sea area, and after the illegal sand mining has been completed, knowing it is illegally excavated sea sand, still directly transships and transports sea sand from the sand mining vessel;

(2) Having prior collusion with the criminal of illegal purchase of sea sand, he directs or pilots the sand transport vessel to the designated sea area to transship and transport sea sand;

(3) There is no evidence of prior collusion or joint criminal intent during the crime among the criminals of illegal excavation, transportation, or purchase of sea sand, but after being employed by one party, he directs or pilots the sand transport vessel to the designated sea area, knowing it is illegally excavated sea sand, still transships and transports sea sand from other sand transport vessels.

II. Determination of Subjective Intent

3. To determine whether the shipowner or captain who transships and transports sea sand has criminal intent, a comprehensive analysis and judgment shall be made based on evidence such as his position, professional experience, professional background, training experience, administrative punishment or criminal liability investigation for similar acts, and in combination with his confession.

In practice, if any of the following circumstances exists and the actor cannot provide a reasonable explanation, it may generally be determined that he "knew it was illegally excavated sea sand", unless there is contrary evidence:

(1) Intentionally turning off the ship's Automatic Identification System (AIS), or having multiple sets of AIS on board, or intentionally destroying or discarding data from onboard satellite phones, AIS, positioning systems, or data stored on mobile phones;

(2) Intentionally detouring from normal routes and docks, transshipping and transporting in concealed waters or at obviously unreasonable concealed times, or using codes, ciphers, tokens, etc. for communication or meeting;

(3) Using "three-no" vessels (no ship name, no certificate of ownership, no valid certificate), vessels with false ship names, or illegally modified vessels, or intentionally concealing the ship number or disguising the hull features;

(4) Falsely recording the ship's deck log or engine log, or failing to declare entry or exit, or making false declarations;

(5) Using legitimate documentation such as relevant permits, auction procedures, contracts, etc. as a cover, or using false or forged documentation;

(6) Unable to produce a lawful and valid certificate of origin for sea sand, or refusing to provide proof of the true origin of sea sand;

(7) Trading at a price significantly below the market price;

(8) The remuneration paid, received, or agreed upon is obviously unreasonable, or using bank accounts under others' names to receive or pay the transaction amount for sea sand;

(9) Evading or resisting law enforcement inspections, or pre-establishing plans to avoid inspection;

(10) Other circumstances sufficient to establish knowledge.

4. If a person, knowing that another person is committing crimes of illegally excavating, transporting or purchasing sea sand, still provides important convenience such as funds, venues, tools, technology, documents, certificates, procedures or intermediary contact, and provides substantial assistance to the crime, he shall be treated as an accomplice in the crime of illegal mining or the crime of concealing or covering up criminal proceeds.

III. Handling of Downstream Acts

5. To determine that illegal transportation, purchase, sale on behalf of, or other methods of concealing or covering up illegally excavated sea sand and the proceeds thereof constitute the crime of concealing or covering up criminal proceeds or proceeds from crime, the premise is that the upstream illegal mining crime is established. If the upstream crime has not yet been adjudicated but has been verified, it does not affect the determination of the crime of concealing or covering up criminal proceeds or proceeds from crime. If the upstream illegal excavation of sea sand does not meet the "serious circumstances" standard for the crime of illegal mining, the corresponding downstream concealment or covering up acts may be subject to administrative penalties in accordance with laws and regulations such as the Marine Environment Protection Law, the Sea Area Use Management Law, and the Public Security Administration Punishments Law.

6. If a person, knowing it is illegally excavated sea sand and the proceeds thereof, transports, purchases, sells on behalf of, or conceals or covers up by other means, and has been subjected to administrative punishment for such acts within one year and then commits such acts again, he shall be convicted and punished for the crime of concealing or covering up criminal proceeds or proceeds from crime in accordance with Article 312 of the Criminal Law. If such acts are committed multiple times without administrative punishment and should be prosecuted according to law, the amount of criminal proceeds and proceeds from crime shall be calculated cumulatively.

7. If a person is convicted and punished for the crime of concealing or covering up criminal proceeds or proceeds from crime, attention shall be paid to maintaining sentencing balance with the upstream illegal mining crime.

IV. Determination of Responsibility of Workers

8. Article 11 of the Illegal Mining Interpretation provides that workers who are employed to provide labor services shall generally not be treated as crimes, except where they participate in profit sharing or receive high fixed wages, except where they have previously been punished for illegal mining or destructive mining. The understanding of "high fixed wages" in this article should not remain at the literal level of "high", but should be comprehensively judged in combination with factors such as the division of duties and degree of participation in the entire criminal activity.

In practice, attention shall be paid to examining and determining in combination with the level of economic and social development of the region and the average wage level of workers providing labor services in the mining industry. Generally, if the fixed property income received or agreed to be received (including wages, bonuses, subsidies, material rewards, etc.) is more than twice the average wage of workers providing labor services in the same type of mining, transportation and other industries in the province (autonomous region or municipality directly under the Central Government) in the previous year, it may be considered as "high fixed wages".

9. If any of the following circumstances exists, the provision of Article 11 of the Illegal Mining Interpretation that "generally not treated as a crime" shall generally not apply:

(1) Knowing that another person is committing crimes of illegally excavating, transporting or purchasing sea sand, he still provides substantial assistance or important technical support such as mining, loading, unloading, transportation, or sales on multiple occasions, and the circumstances are relatively serious;

(2) Undertaking certain responsibilities for initiating, planning, manipulating, managing, or coordinating in the relevant criminal activities;

(3) Evading inspection on multiple occasions, or providing assistance such as tipping off to evade supervision over illegal sea sand excavation activities or to help criminals evade punishment.

V. Determination of the Price of Sea Sand Involved

10. For the value of sea sand involved, if there is an amount of proceeds from selling stolen goods, it is generally determined according to that amount; if there is no amount of proceeds from selling stolen goods, the amount is difficult to verify, or it is obviously unreasonable to determine according to that amount, it shall be determined according to the market transaction price and quantity of sea sand.

Costs incurred in different links of selling illegally excavated sea sand, such as illegal excavation, transportation, and storage, shall not be deducted from the amount of proceeds from selling stolen goods.

11. If the value of sea sand is difficult to determine, it shall be determined based on a report from the price certification agency affiliated with the local government department concerned, or based on a report from the competent department of natural resources, water administration, or ocean at or above the provincial level of the people's government, combined with other evidence.

12. When determining the value of illegally mined sea sand, the market transaction price of local sea sand at the time of the completion of the criminal act or the average market price of local sea sand during the period of illegal excavation shall generally be taken as the benchmark. If the criminal act has obvious continuity over time periods, the market transaction price of local sea sand at the time of committing the criminal act in different periods may be taken as the benchmark respectively. If there is no market transaction price for sea sand in the local county (city, district), the market transaction price of sea sand in surrounding areas may be referred to.

VI. Disposal of Ships and Property Involved

13. For ships involved, the coast guard agency shall promptly seal up and seize them according to law. After seizure, they shall generally be kept by the coast guard agency itself; under special circumstances, they may also be left in the temporary custody of the shipowner or captain.

14. If any of the following circumstances exists, it may generally be considered as "special tools used for crime" as stipulated in Article 12, Paragraph 2 of the Illegal Mining Interpretation, and shall be confiscated according to law:

(1) Tools owned by the criminal and specially used for the crime of illegal sea sand excavation;

(2) Tools that have not been registered for a long time, are "three-no" vessels, or are attached or registered under another's name, but are actually controlled by the criminal and specially used for the crime of illegal sea sand excavation;

(3) The owner of the ship or equipment, knowing that the criminal specially uses it for illegal sea sand excavation, rents or lends the ship or equipment, constituting a joint crime or a related crime.

15. If any of the following circumstances exists, it may generally be considered that the shipowner, knowing that others are specifically using it for illegal excavation and transportation of sea sand, rents or lends the ship, except where a reasonable explanation can be provided or there is contrary evidence:

(1) Without approval from the relevant department, the ship is modified without authorization into a vessel that can be used for excavating or transporting sea sand, or is disguised;

(2) Agreeing to or acquiescing in the modification of the ship by the criminal into a vessel that can be used for excavating or transporting sea sand, or its disguise;

(3) Having previously been subjected to administrative punishment for renting or lending a ship for illegal excavation or transportation of sea sand, and then renting or lending the ship to the same criminal;

(4) Refusing to provide true information on the actual user, or providing false information on the actual user;

(5) Other circumstances sufficient to establish knowledge.

16. If criminals of illegal excavation and transportation of sea sand, in order to avoid the lawful forfeiture of ships specially used for crime, or to avoid being prosecuted for similar acts within one year after being administratively punished for illegal excavation and transportation of sea sand, transfer the ownership of the ship by fictitious sales contracts, oral agreements, etc., but do not register the change in real rights and do not actually pay the transfer price, the ship involved may be determined according to law as a "special tool used for crime".

17. If the value of the ship involved is too disproportionate to the amount involved, and the ship's documents are authentic and valid, the ownership is clear, and the ship matches the documents, it shall generally not be confiscated. In practice, the ship involved shall be disposed of according to law by comprehensively considering factors such as the nature, circumstances, consequences, degree of social harm, and the actor's admission of guilt and repentance.

18. If a shipowner makes a living by illegally transporting sea sand, knowing it is illegally excavated sea sand, and commits illegal transportation of sea sand multiple times within one year, constituting a joint crime or related crime, the ship involved may be determined as "the actor's own property used for crime" as stipulated in Article 12, Paragraph 2 of the Illegal Mining Interpretation, and shall be confiscated according to law.

19. For the sea sand involved that is seized, after securing evidence and retaining samples, the coast guard agency may, with the approval of the principal responsible person of the coast guard agency at or above the county level, auction it first according to law, and conduct full-process supervision of the auction. The proceeds from the auction shall be kept in temporary custody and disposed of according to law after the conclusion of the proceedings.

For the facilities and equipment such as sand mining and transportation machinery on the ship involved, the coast guard agency shall seal up and seize them in a timely manner during the investigation, and the people's court shall in principle decide to confiscate them according to law, or hand them over to the relevant competent department for demolition.

VII. Strengthening Coordination, Cooperation, Supervision and Restraint

20. After a case occurs, the coast guard agency, people's procuratorate, and people's court at the landing place where the criminal suspect or defendant returns from the sea to land may exercise jurisdiction according to law. "Landing place" includes both the "active landing place" where the criminal suspect or defendant returns voluntarily or through other means, and the "passive landing place" where he is escorted back to land by law enforcement departments such as the coast guard agency. The coast guard agency shall choose the landing place in accordance with the principles of nearest landing and facilitation of investigation.

21. People's courts, people's procuratorates, and coast guard agencies at all levels, when handling criminal cases involving sea sand and criminal附带 civil public interest litigation cases, shall give full play to their functions, divide responsibilities, cooperate with each other, restrain each other, and effectively form a joint force to combat. Coast guard agencies at all levels shall strengthen serial case analysis and judgment, focus on in-depth investigation, comprehensively collect, fix and perfect relevant evidence according to law, improve the quality of case handling, and timely request approval of arrest and transfer for review for prosecution. People's procuratorates at all levels shall fully perform their legal supervision duties according to law, and efficiently carry out work such as review and approval of arrest and review for prosecution in criminal cases involving sea sand. When necessary, the people's procuratorate may intervene in the investigation in advance to guide the coast guard agency in comprehensively collecting and fixing evidence for criminal cases and criminal附带 civil public interest litigation cases. People's courts at all levels, when hearing criminal cases involving sea sand, shall effectively perform their trial functions, implement the criminal policy of combining leniency and severity, accurately apply the law, and ensure that the punishment fits the crime.

22. People's courts, people's procuratorates, and coast guard agencies at all levels shall establish and improve institutional mechanisms such as daily contact, information notification, case consultation, and similar case study and judgment, timely study new situations and new problems arising in illegal and criminal activities involving sea sand, solve major, difficult and complex problems, and enhance the effect of case handling.

23. People's courts, people's procuratorates, and coast guard agencies at all levels, when handling criminal cases involving sea sand, shall, in light of their work responsibilities, carefully analyze and study the patterns and causes of illegal and criminal activities involving sea sand, and comprehensively use methods such as issuing judicial recommendations, procuratorial recommendations, carrying out procuratorial public interest litigation, conducting legal publicity, and explaining the law with cases, to build a comprehensive prevention and control system that combines punishment and prevention, prioritizes prevention, and focuses on governance; while focusing on cracking down on crimes, actively promote the governance of sources and comprehensive governance of illegal and criminal activities involving sea sand, cut off the chain of interests, and eradicate the soil that breeds crime.
